  <!-- STONE FIGURINE (2026-08-15). Closes the XML half of the FIGURINE MATERIAL LADDER ruling of
       2026-08-14 (user: "figurines on the floor have a chance to spawn stone (or wood?) instead
       of copper, still giving rep, but very little value"). That ruling was built on the SIM side
       only; the warren lab's own entry still reads "figurine (maquette, mod blueprint TBD)", which
       is sim first working as intended, the lab leading and the XML catching up.
       The engine needed no help. RandomFigurine.Material is a plain string and
       GetFigurineRepValue ends in a default case, so "stone" falls through to DEFAULT_REP_VALUE,
       which is 60: IDENTICAL to copper. So the ruling lands exactly as worded by accident of the
       engine, same social weight, and the "very little value" half is pure Commerce.
       Commerce 3 against copper's 60 is a CLAUDE DRAFT. Display name substitutes *material* so it
       reads "stone <creature> figurine" with no extra work. -->
  <object Name="Cleo_TerraFirma_Random Stone Figurine" Inherits="Random Figurine">
    <part Name="Render" DisplayName="{{y|stone}} {{|*creature*}} figurine" DetailColor="K" RenderString="*" ColorString="&amp;y" RenderLayer="5" />
    <part Name="Commerce" Value="3" />
    <part Name="RandomFigurine" Material="stone" />
    <tag Name="Tier" Value="1" />
  </object>

  <!-- PROPER ETCH TOOLS (2026-08-15, user: "do you think it's a good idea to give them proper
       versions of those tools. maybe copper or steel?"). Rungs RULED steel then carbide at the
       top; the PLAYER KEEPS THE BLUNTED PAIR by the same ruling, which is where its meaning comes
       from: the starter tools are characterised as worn out, and that only reads once the guild
       carries better.
       Copper was considered and dropped. Copper chisels are historically real, Egypt cut limestone
       with them, but copper is SOFTER than what the blunted pair is already made of, and in Qud's
       own ladder copper is figurine and wire material rather than tool material. It would have
       been a downgrade wearing an upgrade's name.
       No new mechanics needed: the etch slip law of 2026-08-07 already reads tool tier, slip
       chance being (6 minus pairTier) times 4 percent, where pair tier is the LESSER of striker
       and edge. So blunted stays at 24% of characters garbled, steel lands 16%, carbide 8%.
       These INHERIT the base tier weapons rather than tagging them, on purpose: tagging Steel
       Dagger directly would quietly make every steel dagger in Qud an etching tool, which is the
       exact widening the rubble cover ruling refused. Narrow tags stay narrow. -->
  <object Name="Cleo_TerraFirma_Steel Chisel" Inherits="Steel Dagger">
    <part Name="Render" DisplayName="steel chisel" Tile="blunted_chisel.png" DetailColor="y" ColorString="&amp;Y" />
    <part Name="Description" Short="A hand's length of steel ground to a chisel edge, its head mushroomed flat from years under the mallet." /> <!-- DRAFT, signoff owed -->
    <tag Name="Cleo_TerraFirma_EtchTool" Value="Chisel" />
  </object>

  <object Name="Cleo_TerraFirma_Steel Mallet" Inherits="Steel Hammer">
    <part Name="Render" DisplayName="steel mallet" Tile="Items/sw_leatherworking_hammer.bmp" DetailColor="y" ColorString="&amp;Y" />
    <part Name="Description" Short="A short steel mallet, its face dented into a shallow bowl by the chisel heads it has driven." /> <!-- DRAFT, signoff owed -->
